+# Full study: Arxiv.«2001.02665».kotzig_conjecture_large
+
+## Frozen object
+
+The target is the exact `kotzig_conjecture_large` declaration from the pinned provider revision `2270d31e8dd611521f979de6d86da364930b7669`, source span `FormalConjectures/Arxiv/2001.02665/RingelConjecture.lean:56-75`. The provider declaration carries `sorryAx`; source bytes are statement authority only.
+
+Its proposition is:
+
+```lean
+∀ᶠ (n : ℕ) in Filter.atTop, ∀ {V : Type} [Finite V] (T : SimpleGraph V),
+  T.IsTree → T.edgeSet.ncard = n →
+  ∃ f : Fin (2 * n + 1) → (V ↪ Fin (2 * n + 1)),
+    (∀ i v, f i v = f 0 v + i) ∧
+    Pairwise (fun i j => Disjoint (T.map (f i)).edgeSet (T.map (f j)).edgeSet) ∧
+    ⨆ i, T.map (f i) = (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin (2 * n + 1)))
+```
+
+## Resolution frontier
+
+The exact statement and both definitional transports are machine-checked. The substantive theorem is a deep cyclic decomposition result (proved externally for sufficiently large n), and no corresponding trust-zero theorem exists in the pinned local libraries. The following anchored fragments separate completed identity work from the unresolved root.
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:BEGIN -->
+### PU-00
+The pinned source record fixes the eventual statement: for all sufficiently large natural n, every finite tree with n edges must admit a single embedding whose 2n+1 cyclic shifts are pairwise edge-disjoint and cover the complete graph. Statement.lean replays that proposition exactly, with Target definitionally equal to FrozenStatement.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:BEGIN -->
+### PU-01
+The outer Filter.Eventually obligation is represented by the canonical Filter.atTop tail. No finite collection of checked values can discharge this tail; a proof must provide one threshold and a uniform construction beyond it.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:BEGIN -->
+### PU-02
+For each tail index n, the local hypotheses are a finite vertex type, T.IsTree, and T.edgeSet.ncard = n. They are sufficient to state the intended decomposition but do not supply a cyclic embedding.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:BEGIN -->
+### PU-03
+The first conjunct requires f i v = f 0 v + i for every shift i and vertex v. Producing a base embedding with this exact modular equivariance is the Kotzig strengthening and is not available in Mathlib.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:BEGIN -->
+### PU-04
+The second conjunct requires Pairwise disjoint edge sets for all shifted copies. This is a global combinatorial packing assertion; it is not implied by IsTree or by the edge-count hypothesis alone.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:BEGIN -->
+### PU-05
+The supremum equality requires the shifted copies to cover every edge of the complete graph on Fin (2*n+1). Edge-count arithmetic is necessary but does not construct the required witnesses or prove equality.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:BEGIN -->
+### PU-06
+A completed root would compose PU-01 through PU-05 into the existential witness for each sufficiently large n. The pinned provider theorem cannot be used for this composition because its body is sorryAx-bearing.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-ROOT-OPEN:BEGIN -->
+### PU-ROOT-OPEN
+The exact machine root remains open. The worker records the missing cyclic tree-decomposition proof as a typed cut rather than claiming theorem completion or importing a bodyless oracle.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-ROOT-OPEN:END -->
+
+## Conclusion
+
+This is a provisional self-tested nonacceptance handoff. `machine_complete` and `theorem_complete` remain false; the canonical Master must preserve the cut and may only accept a future replacement with an independently checked exact root.

